  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some lingering showers through this evening \nbefore turning cooler with less humidity on Monday and Tuesday.\n\nA pre-frontal trough was helping induce a few showers as of \nmid afternoon, but these should be increasingly disorganized \nand isolated/brief in nature. Humidity remains elevated, but \noverall forcing and instability are modest with increasing \nsubsidence aloft.\n\nHeading into tonight, an approaching cold front from the Great\nLakes may induce additional showers along and west of the\nAllegheny Front as weakening storms approach from the Ohio\nValley. This threat should end around or shortly after midnight. \nOtherwise, this is largely a drier frontal passage which will \nalso lead to cool temperatures and lower humidity to start off \nthe work week.\n\nGradients on Monday are rather tight for mid-September as a \nbroad 1024-mb surface high approaches from the Great Lakes. This\nwill favor a breezy northwesterly wind for Monday, with gusts \nup to 20 to 25 mph at times. Forecast highs are in the mid 70s \nto low 80s (5 to 15 degrees cooler for mountain locales).\n\nAs the seasonably strong dome of high pressure settles over the\nnortheastern U.S. Monday night, low temperatures plunge into \nthe upper 40s to 50s. The autumnal feel to the air lingers into \nTuesday before an eventual warm up by mid-week. Plenty of \nsunshine can be expected each day, but with decreasing winds on \nTuesday with the ridge along the Eastern Seaboard. This all \ncomes with dew points in the upper 40s to mid 50s which is \ncertainly a change from recent days.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increasing precipitation chances later in the week \nwith an incoming cold front.\n\nUpper-level ridging could continue into Wednesday, then begin \nto break down as a trough forms over the Hudson Bay. A stemming \ncold front tracks east over the Mid-Atlantic during the later \nhalf of the week, bringing increasing humidity as well as shower\n& thunderstorm chances. There is still some uncertainty in the \ntiming of any precipitation, but generally there should be \nincreasing cloud cover by Thursday with a potential for showers \n& thunderstorms between Thursday and throughout the weekend as \nthe front moves through. Severe probabilities continue to look \nminimal for this period.\n\nTemperatures rise back into the mid-to-upper 80s across the region \nthrough Friday, then drop again in the wake of the front heading \ninto the weekend.",
